## 1. Objective
Prove the whole pipeline end to end (code, tests, contract, running system, deploy) with the smallest possible feature: a service that greets, reports its own health, <%#hasDb%>checks its database, <%/hasDb%><%#hasWeb%>and a web page<%#hasMobile%> and a mobile app that each display<%/hasMobile%><%^hasMobile%> that displays<%/hasMobile%> the greeting from the API.<%/hasWeb%><%^hasWeb%><%#hasMobile%>and a mobile app that displays the greeting from the API.<%/hasMobile%><%^hasMobile%>and a documented API.<%/hasMobile%><%/hasWeb%>

## 2. User stories & acceptance criteria
- **AC-001** Given the backend is running, when a client requests `GET /health`, then it responds `200` with `{"status": "ok"}` without touching any dependency (liveness).
- **AC-002** Given the backend is running, when a client requests `GET /api/v1/hello`, then it responds `200` with `{"message": "Hello, World!"}`.
- **AC-003** Given the backend is running, when a client requests `GET /api/v1/hello?name=Ada`, then it responds `200` with `{"message": "Hello, Ada!"}`.
- **AC-004** Given the backend is running, when a client requests `GET /api/v1/hello` with a `name` longer than 50 characters, then it responds `422`.
<%#hasDb%>
- **AC-005** Given the database is reachable, when a client requests `GET /ready`, then it responds `200` with `{"status": "ok", "database": "up"}`; when the database is unreachable it responds `503` with `{"status": "degraded", "database": "down"}`.
<%/hasDb%>
- **AC-006** The API contract is committed at `.ai/contract/openapi.json` and describes `/health`, `/ready` and `/api/v1/hello`; any drift between code and contract fails the `backend.contract` gate.
<%#hasWeb%>
- **AC-007** Given backend and web are both running, when a user opens the web root `/`, then the page shows the greeting `Hello, World!` fetched from the backend at request time.
<%/hasWeb%>
<%#hasMobile%>
- **AC-008** Given the backend is running, when the mobile app's API client asks for the greeting, then it returns `Hello, World!`, exactly what `GET /api/v1/hello` answers (the client the app's first screen uses, run against the live backend).
<%/hasMobile%>

## 3. Permissions & roles
None: all endpoints are public and read-only.

## 4. Data & validation
- `name`: optional string, 1 to 50 characters, default `World`.
- Response `message`: string, always `Hello, <name>!`.

## 5. Contract impact
New endpoints `GET /health`, `GET /ready`, `GET /api/v1/hello`. No migrations. Error payload for validation failures is FastAPI's standard `422` body.

<%#designEnforced%>
## 6. UI & design
- **Design source**: <%#hasDesignUrl%>`<%designUrl%>`<%/hasDesignUrl%><%^hasDesignUrl%>none: there is no design file yet, so the screen follows the default look in `.ai/design/tokens.json`<%/hasDesignUrl%>.
- **Screens & states**: <%#hasWeb%>the web page is one centred card (the project name, the greeting as its heading, a caption)<%#hasMobile%>; the mobile screen is the same card<%/hasMobile%><%/hasWeb%><%^hasWeb%>the mobile screen is one centred card (the greeting as its heading)<%/hasWeb%><%#hasBackend%>. While the greeting loads the card shows a progress indicator<%#hasWeb%> (mobile)<%/hasWeb%>, and when the backend cannot be reached it shows the reason as an error that is announced to assistive technology<%/hasBackend%><%^hasBackend%>, above a name field<%/hasBackend%>.
- **Components**: `HelloCard`, the only one.
- **Tokens**: none new. The card takes its colours, spacing, radius and type size from `.ai/design/tokens.json`.
- **Copy**: the greeting is `Hello, <name>!`<%#hasBackend%>; the error heading is `Backend unavailable`<%/hasBackend%>.
- **Responsive & accessibility**: <%#hasWeb%>the card is at most the `size.content` token wide and shrinks to fit a narrow screen; <%/hasWeb%>the greeting is a real heading and the colour tokens keep their contrast in the light and the dark theme.

